<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Keys are kept in ALPHABETICAL order, and each key appears exactly ONCE.

  Both rules are load-bearing, not style. This file previously carried ten
  keys twice, from the iOS/Android app split concatenating two templates.
  `plutil -lint` passes on a duplicated plist because CFPropertyList resolves
  duplicates last-wins, so the first copy of every key was silently dead — an
  edit to it did nothing at all. That already bit once: removing iPad
  orientations for the iPhone-only build had to touch BOTH copies, because
  deleting only the first would have been a no-op. It also silently reverted a
  deliberate ATS decision (see NSAppTransportSecurity below).

  A single alphabetical list makes a re-introduced duplicate visible in review.
  Before editing, re-check with:
    plutil -lint Info.plist
    grep -o "<key>[A-Za-z~]*</key>" Info.plist | sort | uniq -d
  (the grep is not nesting-aware — it also flags keys that legitimately repeat
  inside different child dicts, so confirm the level before acting on a hit.)
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>en</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>Companion</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleTypeRole</key>
			<string>Editor</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLName</key>
			<string>com.companionintelligence.app.oidc</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>companion</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>
	<!-- Export compliance. THE STATED BASIS FOR THIS CHANGED AND IS NOT YET CONFIRMED
	     BY COUNSEL - see DELIVERY.md section 1a.

	     The previous basis was "only exempt standard cryptography (HTTPS/TLS via the
	     OS, Keychain)", i.e. no cryptography of the app's own at all. That is no longer
	     true: the app now bundles SQLCipher (AES-256 via CommonCrypto) to encrypt the
	     Local Hub capture outbox at rest, which is app-level crypto rather than an OS
	     service.

	     The likely outcome is still exempt - mass-market software using a standard,
	     published, open-source implementation of a standard algorithm, applied to the
	     app's own data at rest - but "probably exempt on a different basis" is not the
	     claim the old comment made, and `false` here suppresses the App Store Connect
	     encryption questionnaire on every upload. Confirm the determination (and whether
	     an annual self-classification report is owed) before the next submission; set
	     this to `true` if counsel wants the questionnaire answered per-upload instead. -->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>
	<key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
	<true/>
	<!--
	  CI-Server appliances are frequently reached over plain HTTP on a LAN or
	  tailnet (the user's own self-hosted server). Allow cleartext loads so the
	  app can connect to an http:// server entered on the /server screen — the
	  target is always a user-specified, first-party appliance.

	  Scoped to WKWebView content (where all our network requests originate) via
	  NSAllowsArbitraryLoadsInWebContent rather than the app-wide
	  NSAllowsArbitraryLoads, which is the App-Store-preferred, narrower exception.

	  ⚠️ "where all our network requests originate" is NOT true, and has not been
	  since App Intents landed: CompanionAPI (the Siri/"Remember this" client) and
	  CompanionIntentClient both use plain URLSession, which
	  NSAllowsArbitraryLoadsInWebContent does NOT cover — it exempts WKWebView
	  content only. That is why NSAllowsLocalNetworking is also set: it is the
	  documented fix for exactly that gap (DELIVERY.md §1a, "Applied now, narrow"),
	  covering .local and unqualified/private-range LAN hosts, and Apple asks for no
	  review justification for it.

	  It was recorded as applied but was never actually in effect: the copy of this
	  dict that declared it was the dead FIRST copy of a duplicated key, and
	  CFPropertyList takes last-wins. De-duplicating the file exposed that; this
	  restores the decision the doc already records.

	  Still deliberately NOT covered: a public http:// origin, and a Tailscale
	  100.64/10 address (CGNAT, not private-range). Closing those needs app-wide
	  NSAllowsArbitraryLoads — do not add it without the review justification and
	  the Data Safety update called for in DELIVERY.md §1a.
	-->
	<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
	<dict>
		<key>NSAllowsArbitraryLoadsInWebContent</key>
		<true/>
		<key>NSAllowsLocalNetworking</key>
		<true/>
	</dict>
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Companion uses the camera to capture photos and videos for your memory.</string>
	<!--
	  Location — WHEN-IN-USE ONLY, deliberately. Adding
	  NSLocationAlwaysAndWhenInUseUsageDescription (plus UIBackgroundModes: location)
	  is a separate product decision: see DEVICE_DATA_COMPLIANCE.md §3.4 and §7
	  Phase 4. Guideline 5.1.5 requires location be directly relevant to a feature
	  the user can see, so the string names the feature rather than the data.
	-->
	<key>NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Companion records where you are while you use it, so your memory can place your notes, photos and events on a map. Locations are sent only to your own Companion server.</string>
	<key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Companion uses the microphone to record audio and video for your memory.</string>
	<key>NSPhotoLibraryAddUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Companion saves captured media to your photo library when you choose to.</string>
	<key>NSSpeechRecognitionUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Companion turns your speech into text on this device so you can dictate notes and talk to your memory.</string>
	<key>UIApplicationSceneManifest</key>
	<dict>
		<key>UIApplicationSupportsMultipleScenes</key>
		<false/>
		<key>UISceneConfigurations</key>
		<dict>
			<key>UIWindowSceneSessionRoleApplication</key>
			<array>
				<dict>
					<key>UISceneConfigurationName</key>
					<string>Default Configuration</string>
					<key>UISceneDelegateClassName</key>
					<string>$(PRODUCT_MODULE_NAME).SceneDelegate</string>
				</dict>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</dict>
	<key>UILaunchStoryboardName</key>
	<string>LaunchScreen</string>
	<key>UIRequiredDeviceCapabilities</key>
	<array>
		<!-- 64-bit only. `armv7` (the stale Capacitor-template default) is a 32-bit
		     slice no iOS-15 device runs; arm64 is the correct required capability. -->
		<string>arm64</string>
	</array>
	<!-- iPhone-only build (TARGETED_DEVICE_FAMILY = 1). The `~ipad` orientation
	     variants were deliberately REMOVED as dead weight, not because they were
	     doing anything: iPad compatibility and App Review device assignment are
	     driven solely by TARGETED_DEVICE_FAMILY, and iOS never reads a `~ipad`
	     variant on an iPhone-only binary. They are gone so the plist and the build
	     setting do not disagree. On iPad this ships as an iPhone-compatibility app
	     (Apple's own scaled phone window), which the @ci/fe-web mobile layout is
	     actually designed for. To reverse: set TARGETED_DEVICE_FAMILY = "1,2" in
	     BOTH the Debug and Release configs of the App target (the plist alone will
	     not do it), restore the `~ipad` orientation key here, and add a tablet tier
	     to the layout hooks — see DELIVERY.md §1a. -->
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
	<key>UIViewControllerBasedStatusBarAppearance</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
